NAME
    rm - remove writable filesystem objects

SYNOPSIS
    rm FILE...
    rm -r DIRECTORY...
    rm -R DIRECTORY...

DESCRIPTION
    Removes each operand in turn from a writable provider. Removing one ext4
    hard-link name preserves the inode and its other names. With -r or -R,
    traverses directories iteratively in post-order and never follows symbolic
    links. Roots, mountpoints, immutable content, and mounted read-only content
    are rejected.

    Every operand is attempted. A failed operand is reported and the remaining
    operands are still removed; the exit status reflects the first failure. An
    optional -- ends option parsing, so a name beginning with - can be removed.

    The shell expands patterns before rm runs, so rm never sees a pattern. See
    sh for the expansion rules and their bounds.

EXAMPLES
    rm notes.txt
    rm *.txt
    rm -r /vol/shared/build
